WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.960 --> 00:00:08.000
On the 29th of April 1868, the United States&nbsp;
government signed the Treaty of Fort Laramie&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:08.000 --> 00:00:13.840
with several leaders of the Sioux tribes&nbsp;
of the Great Plains. By November many of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:13.840 --> 00:00:20.000
the older leaders of the Sioux like Red&nbsp;
Cloud had agreed to its terms and went to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:20.000 --> 00:00:27.280
live on fed-eral-run reservations. One younger&nbsp;
leader was militant in his resistance though.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:27.280 --> 00:00:33.920
Eight years later Sitting Bull would win the&nbsp;
most famous Sioux victory over the US. How&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:33.920 --> 00:00:40.560
did this young member of the Hunkpapa tribe go&nbsp;
from local tribal leader to the victor of the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:40.560 --> 00:00:52.960
Battle of the Little Bighorn? This is the story&nbsp;
of Sitting Bull and the Great Sioux War. 
	
&nbsp;

00:01:01.120 --> 00:01:10.880
The man known to history as Sitting Bull was born&nbsp;
sometime between 1831 and 1837 in Many Caches,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:10.880 --> 00:01:17.040
a collection of native American storage pits&nbsp;
located in Dakota. He was born into a noble&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:17.040 --> 00:01:24.400
family of the Hunkpapa, a sub-tribe of the Lakota&nbsp;
people. The Lakota and the Dakota collectively&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:24.400 --> 00:01:29.680
made up the Sioux Native Americans that dominated&nbsp;
the region from the western edges of the Great&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:29.680 --> 00:01:36.080
Lakes around Minnesota all the way through the&nbsp;
Great Plains and the Dakotas to Montana. Sitting&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:36.080 --> 00:01:43.040
Bull had several names during his life. For&nbsp;
instance, he was known as Ȟoká Psíče, meaning&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:43.040 --> 00:01:49.440
‘Jumping Badger’ in his childhood years.&nbsp;
Later he acquired the name Tȟatȟáŋka Íyotake,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:49.440 --> 00:01:55.760
which translates as ‘Sitting Bull’. 
Sitting Bull’s father was also called Sitting&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:55.760 --> 00:02:02.320
Bull. At the time of his son’s birth, he was chief&nbsp;
of the Hunkpapa tribe along with his two brothers,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:02.320 --> 00:02:08.880
Four Horns and Looks-for-Him-in-a-Tent.&nbsp;
Sitting Bull’s mother was Her-Holy-Door.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:08.880 --> 00:02:16.720
He had two known sisters, Good Feather and Twin&nbsp;
Woman, as well as one half-brother, Fool Dog, who&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:16.720 --> 00:02:25.840
lived with his mother’s clan, the Arikaras. 
By the age of ten, Jumping Badger was recognised&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:25.840 --> 00:02:31.520
as an accomplished hunter, having already&nbsp;
killed his first buffalo. Four years later,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:31.520 --> 00:02:36.320
the young boy’s martial talents helped him&nbsp;
attain his first battlefield kill against&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:36.320 --> 00:02:43.520
his people’s most hated rivals, the Crows.&nbsp;
In 1845 the Hunkpapa were engaged in a border&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:43.520 --> 00:02:50.560
war with the Crows. On the third day the war&nbsp;
party ambushed a band of a dozen Crow riders,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:50.560 --> 00:02:56.400
and in the ensuing melee Jumping Badger chased&nbsp;
down a mounted enemy and knocked him off his&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:56.400 --> 00:03:02.640
horse with a tomahawk. Returning victorious to&nbsp;
their camp, a white eagle feather representing&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:02.640 --> 00:03:09.200
bravery was placed in his hair to symbolise his&nbsp;
transition to manhood. His body was painted black&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:09.200 --> 00:03:15.600
from head-to-toe and he was led around the camp&nbsp;
to rapturous applause. Then in the final act&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:15.600 --> 00:03:21.840
Jumping Badger was renamed Sitting Bull by his&nbsp;
father, who also gifted his son a traditional&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:21.840 --> 00:03:28.400
buffalo-hide shield he would use for the rest of&nbsp;
his life. Eager to grow his standing as a master&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:28.400 --> 00:03:35.200
warrior, he took part in numerous successful war&nbsp;
expeditions throughout the 1850s that expanded&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:35.200 --> 00:03:41.440
Hunkpapa territory. During these skirmishes he&nbsp;
won about a dozen one-on-one battles, all of which&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:41.440 --> 00:03:48.480
were independently observed and recorded by other&nbsp;
men. Eventually his name struck so much fear that&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:48.480 --> 00:03:55.280
his comrades would shout “We are Sitting Bull’s&nbsp;
boys” to demoralise the enemy before they had&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:55.280 --> 00:04:03.280
even started fighting. His insatiable desire for&nbsp;
glory however would come at a price in 1856 when,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:03.280 --> 00:04:10.000
challenged by a foe to a one-to-one duel with&nbsp;
muskets, he was shot by a bullet in the foot.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:10.000 --> 00:04:17.600
Despite killing his opponent, he would live for&nbsp;
the rest of his life with a limp. 
&nbsp;

00:04:17.600 --> 00:04:24.480
In this period Sitting Bull not only excelled as&nbsp;
a soldier, but also as a mystic. In the same year&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:24.480 --> 00:04:30.800
of his injury he became a Wicasa Wakan, which&nbsp;
translates as ‘sacred man’ and which according&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:30.800 --> 00:04:37.600
to Lakota custom was an individual who had the&nbsp;
ability to have dream visions. In order to attain&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:37.600 --> 00:04:43.760
this spiritual status Sitting Bull’s chest and&nbsp;
back muscles were skewered and he was hung in&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:43.760 --> 00:04:50.400
the air until he was able to tear them loose&nbsp;
in a ritual known as a sun-dance sacrifice.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:50.400 --> 00:04:55.840
The following year, after being recognised as&nbsp;
a man that exemplified the Lakota virtues of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:55.840 --> 00:05:02.240
bravery and wisdom, he was made war chief of the&nbsp;
Hunkpapa tribe in a ceremony that took place at&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:02.240 --> 00:05:08.880
the mouth of the Grand River, near his birthplace.&nbsp;
As part of the tribal rites, Sitting Bull smoked a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:08.880 --> 00:05:15.680
pipe made from porcupine quills and duck feathers&nbsp;
and drank a cup containing water and sweetgrass,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:15.680 --> 00:05:21.920
before being presented with a cane that symbolised&nbsp;
long life. Sitting Bull’s father was killed around&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:21.920 --> 00:05:29.040
this time, in 1859, while fighting against the&nbsp;
hated Crows. Having seen his father’s dead corpse,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:29.040 --> 00:05:34.960
the furious Sitting Bull chased the culprit&nbsp;
down and killed him to exact revenge.
&nbsp;

00:05:38.000 --> 00:05:45.040
Sitting Bull’s ascension to leadership coincided&nbsp;
with the rise of the Wasichus, the Lakota term for&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:45.040 --> 00:05:51.680
the new settlers, who at the end of the 1850s&nbsp;
were emerging as the single biggest threat to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:51.680 --> 00:05:58.240
his tribe’s existence. After decades of massive&nbsp;
settlement around the Great Lakes, the influx of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:58.240 --> 00:06:04.160
hundreds of thousands of immigrants into the US&nbsp;
from Europe every year was now fuelling American&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:04.160 --> 00:06:12.160
expansion further westward again towards the Great&nbsp;
Plains in the 1850s and 1860s. More and more of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:12.160 --> 00:06:18.720
these settlers began moving in and encroaching on&nbsp;
Lakota lands bringing with them disease, alcohol,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:18.720 --> 00:06:27.040
and livestock that ate the prairie grasses where&nbsp;
wild buffalo fed. In March 1856 the power dynamic&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:27.040 --> 00:06:33.120
between the newcomers and natives was starkly&nbsp;
outlined by General William Harney when he&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:33.120 --> 00:06:39.280
gathered Lakota tribe leaders to inform them of&nbsp;
a treaty of his own making that had three major&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:39.280 --> 00:06:46.560
stipulations. The first was that native Americans&nbsp;
who had committed crimes against settlers were to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:46.560 --> 00:06:53.920
be handed over to US authorities, the second was&nbsp;
that stolen property must be returned to military&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:53.920 --> 00:07:01.120
outposts, and the third was that they were not&nbsp;
to loiter near roads used by settlers nor were&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:01.120 --> 00:07:06.880
they to obstruct any non Native American&nbsp;
travellers. In return the general pledged&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:06.880 --> 00:07:13.360
that they would enjoy the protection of the US&nbsp;
government, yet this was a treaty in name only.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:13.360 --> 00:07:19.920
The US Senate had not ratified it and it had no&nbsp;
legal basis. Most of the Lakota and Dakota people&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:19.920 --> 00:07:25.920
simply chose to ignore it, leading to a surge in&nbsp;
violent confrontations between the two races in&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:25.920 --> 00:07:32.400
what are now Minnesota and Iowa, west into the&nbsp;
Dakotas and Nebraska. 
&nbsp;

00:07:34.000 --> 00:07:40.960
Tensions between the various Sioux tribes and&nbsp;
the Euro-American settlers intensified in 1862&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:40.960 --> 00:07:46.560
when the discovery of gold in the Rocky Mountains&nbsp;
transformed the Missouri River and surrounding&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:46.560 --> 00:07:52.720
areas into a popular transportation hub for miners&nbsp;
heading westwards, leading to further encroachment&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:52.720 --> 00:07:58.960
on the lands of the Lakota and Dakota. Within a&nbsp;
very short space of time the Missouri River was&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:58.960 --> 00:08:06.800
riddled with prospectors, steam-boat captains&nbsp;
and miners. The Dakota War of 1862 marked the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:06.800 --> 00:08:13.040
beginning of more intense clashes between the US&nbsp;
government and the various Sioux tribes as huge&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:13.040 --> 00:08:19.600
numbers of Dakota tribespeople were displaced from&nbsp;
Minnesota and pushed into the Dakota Territory.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:19.600 --> 00:08:26.000
Others were rounded up and sent to reservations in&nbsp;
Nebraska. Sitting Bull and his followers had their&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:26.000 --> 00:08:32.000
power base further west and were only peripherally&nbsp;
involved in the Dakota War, but they would have&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:32.000 --> 00:08:38.240
been completely aware of the implications of this&nbsp;
westward expansion of the United States. The next&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:38.240 --> 00:08:43.760
year the invaders signalled their intention to&nbsp;
permanently stay in the area with the construction&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:43.760 --> 00:08:50.160
of Fort Sully, located a couple of miles above&nbsp;
Fort Pierre on the west bank of the Missouri.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:50.160 --> 00:08:56.800
This set the stage for the Battle of Kildeer&nbsp;
Mountain at the end of July 1864, where the gulf&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:56.800 --> 00:09:03.680
in technology between the two sides was evident&nbsp;
as the US forces used accurate long-range rifles,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:03.680 --> 00:09:11.840
cannons, and artillery. By 1865 Sitting Bull’s&nbsp;
attitude towards the settlers was clear among&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:11.840 --> 00:09:18.320
his compatriots. With the notable exception of&nbsp;
traders who sold guns to his people, he believed&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:18.320 --> 00:09:25.760
that all other non Native Americans must leave&nbsp;
Lakota territory and never return. Over the next&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:25.760 --> 00:09:32.000
four years the chief practised what he preached&nbsp;
by leading the offensive against the newcomers.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:32.000 --> 00:09:37.760
Others amongst the Lakota, having witnessed the&nbsp;
superiority of the invaders on the battlefield,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:37.760 --> 00:09:44.000
favoured a more diplomatic approach and believed&nbsp;
that negotiating with the settlers was the only&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:44.000 --> 00:09:50.720
realistic survival option. This difference in&nbsp;
opinion caused fractures within the Lakota best&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:50.720 --> 00:09:56.240
exemplified by Sitting Bull’s multiple raids&nbsp;
on the military stronghold of Fort Rice in&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:56.240 --> 00:10:03.280
the spring of 1865. As more and more of his&nbsp;
countrymen bent the knee to the invaders,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:03.280 --> 00:10:10.800
Sitting Bull stepped up his resistance throughout&nbsp;
1866 and 1867, focusing his military efforts on&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:10.800 --> 00:10:16.880
another installation called Fort Buford which,&nbsp;
built deep in Lakota territory, represented&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:16.880 --> 00:10:24.000
the most egregious incursion yet. To the west,&nbsp;
his ally, Red Cloud, harassed the newly-erected&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:24.000 --> 00:10:30.000
US military forts. Red Cloud, however, would&nbsp;
eventually give in the following year when&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:30.000 --> 00:10:38.080
he signed a peace deal that put an end to his&nbsp;
guerrilla campaign in the Northern Plains. 
&nbsp;

00:10:38.080 --> 00:10:43.360
Having proven time and time again his&nbsp;
determination to rid his domains of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:43.360 --> 00:10:49.200
settlers, and as a highly-respected Lakota&nbsp;
chieftain who was still relatively young,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:49.200 --> 00:10:55.600
Sitting Bull was deemed the perfect candidate for&nbsp;
a new position concocted by his uncle, Four Horns,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:55.600 --> 00:11:01.200
that broke with tradition. With the plight&nbsp;
of his people only worsening year after year,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:01.200 --> 00:11:06.320
Four Horns proposed that his nephew become&nbsp;
the head chief of a Sioux Confederation that&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:06.320 --> 00:11:12.960
would unite the Lakota and Dakota tribes under one&nbsp;
banner. This coalition of survival came into being&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:12.960 --> 00:11:21.200
in 1869 when, in the middle of Rosebud Creek,&nbsp;
the Hunkpapas, Blackfeet, Miniconjous, Sans Arcs,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:21.200 --> 00:11:26.640
Oglalas, and Cheyennes elected Sitting&nbsp;
Bull as the chief of their confederacy,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:26.640 --> 00:11:32.880
a position which gave him authority to make war&nbsp;
and peace. His ascension to this position also&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:32.880 --> 00:11:38.880
marked a decisive shift away from the aggression&nbsp;
that had characterised much of the 1860s to a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:38.880 --> 00:11:45.280
more defensive strategy. Having been the spear of&nbsp;
his people Sitting Bull now became their shield,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:45.280 --> 00:11:50.640
withdrawing from direct attacks on forts and&nbsp;
instead prioritising the retention of their&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:50.640 --> 00:11:59.040
remaining territory. Soon enough they would be&nbsp;
drawn back into conflict though. 
	
&nbsp;

00:11:59.040 --> 00:12:04.000
Since the Hunkpapas were practitioners&nbsp;
of polygamy, Sitting Bull had many wives&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:04.000 --> 00:12:10.640
throughout his life including Light Hair,&nbsp;
Snow-on-Her, and Red Woman or Scarlet Woman,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:10.640 --> 00:12:17.280
with the latter two becoming bitter rivals.&nbsp;
In 1872 he wed the sisters Four Robes and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:17.280 --> 00:12:24.640
Seen-By-Her-Nation. He had six daughters, male&nbsp;
twins, as well as two adopted sons, One Bull and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:24.640 --> 00:12:31.440
Jumping Bull. In appearance Sitting Bull was just&nbsp;
under six feet tall and, though he had a solid,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:31.440 --> 00:12:38.320
muscular build, he was much more agile than&nbsp;
met the eye. He had striking eyes, a flat nose,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:38.320 --> 00:12:45.040
thin lips, and a large head with long dark hair&nbsp;
which, often braided to one side with otter fur,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:45.040 --> 00:12:51.200
went down to his shoulders. In personality he&nbsp;
was famously generous, spending later earnings&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:51.200 --> 00:12:58.240
he got from his celebrity status on lavish feasts&nbsp;
for his friends and kinsmen. Supposedly able to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:58.240 --> 00:13:04.640
communicate and talk with them in their song,&nbsp;
he was so fond of birds that one time he even&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:04.640 --> 00:13:11.680
stopped a feast to fix up a wounded meadowlark. A&nbsp;
nature lover, he was also a man of many talents,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:11.680 --> 00:13:17.680
and, aside from being a feared warrior and&nbsp;
respected war chief, was a champion sprinter,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:17.680 --> 00:13:24.800
a knowledgeable healer and a revered holy man who&nbsp;
frequently experienced prophetic visions. 
&nbsp;

00:13:26.480 --> 00:13:31.920
As America grew, so did its transport&nbsp;
system, leading to plans for a Northern&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:31.920 --> 00:13:37.360
Pacific Railway that would cut right through&nbsp;
the heart of the Lakota territory. Swapping&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:37.360 --> 00:13:43.120
their old powder muskets for superior rifles&nbsp;
with cartridge bullets, indigenous resistance&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:43.120 --> 00:13:49.120
against the railroad intensified in the early&nbsp;
1870s, with nighttime assaults on encampments&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:49.120 --> 00:13:54.480
of railroad engineers on the north bank of the&nbsp;
Yellowstone River which cut through the sacred&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:54.480 --> 00:14:01.520
Elk Valley of the Lakota homeland. According to&nbsp;
Lakota lore, during one battle at Arrow Creek,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:01.520 --> 00:14:08.480
Sitting Bull took some tobacco, his pipe, bow, and&nbsp;
rifle, and calmly walked straight into the middle&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:08.480 --> 00:14:14.880
of the contested, open plains. Then, with enemy&nbsp;
bullets whistling past or hitting the dirt around&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:14.880 --> 00:14:23.280
him, he sat down, lit his pipe, and exclaimed:&nbsp;
“Who other Indians with to smoke with me come”&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:23.280 --> 00:14:28.960
upon which four of his compatriots joined&nbsp;
the smoking party. After they had finished&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:28.960 --> 00:14:35.600
the tobacco bowl and Sitting Bull had cleaned his&nbsp;
pipe, his companions rushed back to safety but the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:35.600 --> 00:14:42.240
Sioux chieftain didn’t follow their lead, instead&nbsp;
taking his time to get up and walk serenely back&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:42.240 --> 00:14:49.600
to friendly lines. Despite his bravery, Sitting&nbsp;
Bull’s war bands inflicted very few casualties&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:49.600 --> 00:14:55.120
against the better armed and organised railroad&nbsp;
expedition, which they shadowed for a week or&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:55.120 --> 00:15:01.280
so after the initial fighting at Arrow Creek.&nbsp;
With plans for the railway still in full swing&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:01.280 --> 00:15:07.680
despite Siting Bull’s opposition, the next year&nbsp;
another survey expedition set off towards the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:07.680 --> 00:15:14.480
Yellowstone. Leading the Seventh Cavalry was&nbsp;
Lieutenant Colonel George Armstrong Custer,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:14.480 --> 00:15:20.560
who was nicknamed Long Hair for the distinctive&nbsp;
red-gold hair that tumbled down to just above&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:20.560 --> 00:15:27.840
his shoulders. In early August 1873, from the&nbsp;
time his soldiers were spotted by the Sioux,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:27.840 --> 00:15:34.320
Custer pursued a series of inconclusive skirmishes&nbsp;
with the Lakota, a stark contrast to what would&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:34.320 --> 00:15:40.000
happen during their second meeting years later.&nbsp;
For now though, the natives held their ground&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:40.000 --> 00:15:45.360
in what would turn out to be their last battle&nbsp;
with the railroad companies and their protection&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:45.360 --> 00:15:52.800
escorts for some time. In 1873 an economic&nbsp;
crash occurred in the US owing to excessive&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:52.800 --> 00:16:00.080
speculation on the railways. American and European&nbsp;
economic markets were crippled for several years,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:16:00.080 --> 00:16:05.920
literally stopping the development of the Northern&nbsp;
Pacific Railway in its tracks.
&nbsp;

00:16:07.920 --> 00:16:14.720
With the pace of non Native American expansion&nbsp;
temporarily slowing down, over the next few years&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:16:14.720 --> 00:16:21.200
Sitting Bull intensified his military campaigns&nbsp;
against the Crows who were one of the tribes who,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:16:21.200 --> 00:16:27.520
having accepted the 1868 treaty, had allowed the&nbsp;
settlers to establish a reservation and support&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:16:27.520 --> 00:16:34.480
agencies on their territory. Simultaneously&nbsp;
he also repulsed an invasion of around 150&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:16:34.480 --> 00:16:42.240
prospectors who had trespassed on his homeland&nbsp;
in February 1874 to look for sources of gold.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:16:42.240 --> 00:16:48.000
By this point Sitting Bull’s consistent hostility&nbsp;
had made it clear that he would never willingly&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:16:48.000 --> 00:16:54.800
acquiesce to US rule, fuelling speculation that a&nbsp;
bigger, more devastating war was inevitable. The&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:16:54.800 --> 00:17:02.560
Great Sioux War of 1876 kicked off when Sitting&nbsp;
Bull refused to sell a sacred piece of his domain&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:17:02.560 --> 00:17:09.280
called the Black Hills to the US government, who&nbsp;
wanted to mine it for gold. On the 17th of March&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:17:09.280 --> 00:17:16.320
1876 in snowy and icy conditions, American troops&nbsp;
launched a surprise attack against the Northern&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:17:16.320 --> 00:17:22.560
Cheyenne tribe on the Powder River in the Montana&nbsp;
Territory, during which they burned down half&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:17:22.560 --> 00:17:27.920
of their camp, leaving them without shelter and&nbsp;
provisions and forcing them to find refuge with&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:17:27.920 --> 00:17:34.320
Sitting Bull, who offered it generously. With&nbsp;
the enemy closing in, Sitting Bull appealed to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:17:34.320 --> 00:17:40.960
the spiritual world to guide him and in June,&nbsp;
after completing sacred rites over four days he&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:17:40.960 --> 00:17:47.760
was suddenly struck by a profound vision of the&nbsp;
invaders turned upside down. To Sitting Bull and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:17:47.760 --> 00:17:56.560
those who deciphered it, this was a portent&nbsp;
of a great victory to come very soon.
&nbsp;

00:17:56.560 --> 00:18:05.040
On the 17th of May 1876, Lieutenant Colonel Custer&nbsp;
had led a column of 750 men out of Fort Abraham&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:18:05.040 --> 00:18:11.680
Lincoln with the intention of destroying Sitting&nbsp;
Bull’s forces for good. Aided by Crow scouts,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:18:11.680 --> 00:18:18.080
the plan was for Custer to march up the Rosebud&nbsp;
River to Sitting Bull’s village and attack it.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:18:18.080 --> 00:18:23.920
After overpowering them, the Sioux were expected&nbsp;
to flee north where a force overseen by generals&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:18:23.920 --> 00:18:30.960
Alfred Terry and John Gibbon would block their&nbsp;
path and finish them off. By the 24th of June,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:18:30.960 --> 00:18:36.160
Custer knew that his force had already been&nbsp;
spotted by Lakota scouts who had been observing&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:18:36.160 --> 00:18:41.520
them from a distance. Fearing that the warriors&nbsp;
in the village he intended to take would scatter&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:18:41.520 --> 00:18:48.080
out in all directions, he then made the fateful&nbsp;
decision to attack on the afternoon of the 25th,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:18:48.080 --> 00:18:54.160
rather than waiting until the morning of the&nbsp;
26th. In doing so, Custer was essentially going&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:18:54.160 --> 00:19:00.240
in blind because the size of Sitting Bull’s&nbsp;
war party was unknown. Then, on the afternoon&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:19:00.240 --> 00:19:06.000
of the 25th of June, as the Lakota hurriedly&nbsp;
painted themselves and readied their weapons,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:19:06.000 --> 00:19:12.320
Custer made another tactical blunder when he&nbsp;
divided his regiment into three separate groups,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:19:12.320 --> 00:19:18.960
meaning each was isolated from the rest.&nbsp;
Custer’s cavalry, led by Major Marcus Reno,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:19:18.960 --> 00:19:24.720
were the first to charge down into the southern&nbsp;
end of the village. As they let off their first&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:19:24.720 --> 00:19:31.200
volleys of bullets, Sitting Bull hurriedly donned&nbsp;
his war gear while his stepson, One Bull, handed&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:19:31.200 --> 00:19:37.440
him a Winchester repeating rifle and a six-shooter&nbsp;
pistol. As the horsemen approached the first&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:19:37.440 --> 00:19:44.720
Hunkpapa tipis just before a river-bend, some&nbsp;
of them dismounted, forming a thin static line&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:19:44.720 --> 00:19:51.520
of fire. After advancing about a hundred yards&nbsp;
they dropped to their bellies on open ground.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:19:51.520 --> 00:19:56.560
But the return fire was so intense that the&nbsp;
soldiers were forced to retreat into the cover&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:19:56.560 --> 00:20:03.280
of a thicket of forest to the right. With the&nbsp;
momentum shifting in the Hunkpapa’s favour, a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:20:03.280 --> 00:20:09.920
squadron of mounted horsemen led by Sitting Bull’s&nbsp;
staunch ally, Crazy Horse, swarmed the enemy&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:20:09.920 --> 00:20:16.880
position from all directions forcing them to fall&nbsp;
back to a clearing in the woods. Their unity lost,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:20:16.880 --> 00:20:23.200
isolated pockets of Custer’s soldiers hid&nbsp;
and fired from behind bushes. In utter panic,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:20:23.200 --> 00:20:29.600
the American troopers who weren’t being cut down&nbsp;
by tomahawks and rifles either fled on horses&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:20:29.600 --> 00:20:36.320
across the south-east plain or were washed away by&nbsp;
the strong river current along with their steeds.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:20:36.320 --> 00:20:44.160
100 or so survivors out of 175 scrabbled to a&nbsp;
high point to the east of the river as their&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:20:44.160 --> 00:20:52.800
wounded comrades were killed below. 
As this was happening, Custer and his division&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:20:52.800 --> 00:20:58.800
were approaching the village from the north.&nbsp;
He stationed his three regiments of 210 men&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:20:58.800 --> 00:21:05.280
in the upper part of Medicine Tail. Expecting&nbsp;
to see his commander Frederick Benteen arrive,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:21:05.280 --> 00:21:11.200
he was instead met by the horde of Sioux warriors&nbsp;
who had made their way north. For a short time,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:21:11.200 --> 00:21:17.840
Custer’s men held off the encroaching warriors,&nbsp;
then one massive native charge panicked the horses&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:21:17.840 --> 00:21:23.840
into a great stampede. Galloping in the direction&nbsp;
of the river, they were caught by Cheyenne women&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:21:23.840 --> 00:21:29.200
who emptied their saddlebags of ammunition&nbsp;
and tossed them to the attacking warriors.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:21:29.200 --> 00:21:35.280
The panicked columns split into two, with those&nbsp;
in the south cut down individually or in groups&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:21:35.280 --> 00:21:40.480
while the other stragglers, including Custer,&nbsp;
scrambled to a hilltop at the end of a ridge&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:21:40.480 --> 00:21:48.480
to make their last stand. There he and his&nbsp;
men met their demise in a flurry of dust,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:21:48.480 --> 00:21:54.560
smoke and hand-to-hand fighting, with the&nbsp;
last of them finished off by a final assault.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:21:54.560 --> 00:22:01.200
The other regiments led by Reno and Benteen were&nbsp;
the last to be defeated at dawn on the 26th of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:22:01.200 --> 00:22:09.680
June. Broken and demoralised, Sitting Bull allowed&nbsp;
them to retreat. In total nearly half of Custer’s&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:22:09.680 --> 00:22:15.600
700-strong regiment had been killed or wounded,&nbsp;
in what had been without doubt the greatest&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:22:15.600 --> 00:22:22.240
military triumph in their history and an emphatic&nbsp;
fulfilment of Sitting Bull’s dream vision. Sitting&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:22:22.240 --> 00:22:27.760
Bull had spent most of the battle observing and&nbsp;
tending to the wounded, a not unusual thing for&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:22:27.760 --> 00:22:35.440
a leader in his forties who was beyond the&nbsp;
peak of his fighting abilities.    
&nbsp;

00:22:35.440 --> 00:22:41.440
Following this victory, the Sioux confederacy&nbsp;
was at the pinnacle of its powers, but they&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:22:41.440 --> 00:22:48.160
had also spelled their own destruction as the&nbsp;
horrified American public demanded vengeance. In&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:22:48.160 --> 00:22:53.520
the aftermath of Little Bighorn, several small&nbsp;
and mostly inconclusive battles were fought&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:22:53.520 --> 00:22:59.600
between the Sioux and the Americans in and around&nbsp;
Yellowstone. The increased presence of government&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:22:59.600 --> 00:23:05.280
soldiers and the planned construction of permanent&nbsp;
forts in the middle of hunting ranges was designed&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:23:05.280 --> 00:23:11.840
to starve the Native Americans out by disrupting&nbsp;
their main food supply, the buffalo herds.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:23:11.840 --> 00:23:17.840
This proved so effective and demoralising that&nbsp;
the Sioux Confederacy began to break apart,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:23:17.840 --> 00:23:24.240
with the Sans Arc and Miniconjou chiefs the&nbsp;
first major groups to defect from the alliance,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:23:24.240 --> 00:23:30.000
giving up their freedom in exchange for life on&nbsp;
government-run reservations. During the freezing&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:23:30.000 --> 00:23:38.560
winter of 1876 to 1877, after being identified&nbsp;
by the US media as the evil savage responsible&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:23:38.560 --> 00:23:44.880
for Custer’s fall, the American military attempted&nbsp;
to hunt down Sitting Bull and his shrinking circle&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:23:44.880 --> 00:23:52.000
of allies. The Sioux were chased from camp to&nbsp;
camp, their supplies and shelters set aflame. In&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:23:52.000 --> 00:24:00.320
May 1877, after a traumatic winter, he and his&nbsp;
men crossed the northern frontier into Canada&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:24:00.320 --> 00:24:06.320
to seek refuge. Sitting Bull quickly became&nbsp;
acquainted with the North-West Mounted Police,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:24:06.320 --> 00:24:12.000
who accepted their presence on the condition that&nbsp;
they respect their laws, one of which would strip&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:24:12.000 --> 00:24:18.400
away their right to asylum if they went over&nbsp;
the frontier and back into the USA. At first&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:24:18.400 --> 00:24:23.520
assured by the Canadians they would protect&nbsp;
them from any cross-border American attack,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:24:23.520 --> 00:24:29.440
the decision to come to a territory known as&nbsp;
the Grandmother seemed like a wise one. Yet&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:24:29.440 --> 00:24:34.880
while the elders of the Sioux were willing to&nbsp;
follow Canadian law, hot-blooded youngsters of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:24:34.880 --> 00:24:41.120
their clan disregarded it, continuing hostilities&nbsp;
with other tribes or American troops and hunting&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:24:41.120 --> 00:24:47.440
buffalo on the other side of the border. Thus,&nbsp;
soon enough, diplomatic tensions between the US&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:24:47.440 --> 00:24:54.800
and the Canadians escalated over the fugitives&nbsp;
and a big diplomatic row erupted. Still, during&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:24:54.800 --> 00:25:03.040
a meeting with American envoys sent to urge them&nbsp;
to surrender, on the 16th of October 1877, Sitting&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:25:03.040 --> 00:25:09.840
Bull made it clear that he would not return to&nbsp;
the US under any conditions. 
&nbsp;

00:25:11.520 --> 00:25:17.440
Sitting Bull and his men now attempted to settle&nbsp;
more permanently in the Prairies of Canada,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:25:17.440 --> 00:25:22.480
busying themselves with hunting innumerable&nbsp;
buffalo herds that roamed freely north of the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:25:22.480 --> 00:25:29.280
border. In the spring of 1878, his ranks were&nbsp;
swelled with the addition of more refugees, the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:25:29.280 --> 00:25:35.920
total number of indigenous immigrants increasing&nbsp;
to roughly 6,500 people. With so many more mouths&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:25:35.920 --> 00:25:41.760
to feed the once flourishing buffalo herds,&nbsp;
which also fed the indigenous Canadian tribes,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:25:41.760 --> 00:25:47.280
began to reduce in size dramatically, creating&nbsp;
tensions between the newcomers and the natives&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:25:47.280 --> 00:25:53.360
here north of the border. In response, Sitting&nbsp;
Bull and his men began raiding south of the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:25:53.360 --> 00:26:01.760
border again. During one of these illegal,&nbsp;
southward migrations, on the 17th of July 1879,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:26:01.760 --> 00:26:09.040
Sitting Bull and 600 of his followers were caught&nbsp;
by surprise by an American force led by General&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:26:09.040 --> 00:26:15.520
Nelson Miles. During the Battle of Milk River,&nbsp;
as both parties took potshots at each other,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:26:15.520 --> 00:26:20.720
a senior Crow commander called Magpie,&nbsp;
who was allied with the US contingent,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:26:20.720 --> 00:26:27.440
rode out and challenged Sitting Bull to single&nbsp;
combat, which he accepted. Riding their horses&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:26:27.440 --> 00:26:34.160
towards each other with rifles cocked, Magpie’s&nbsp;
weapon jammed and misfired and an instant later&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:26:34.160 --> 00:26:40.560
the crown of his head was blown off by a deadeye&nbsp;
shot from Sitting Bull, who cut off what was left&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:26:40.560 --> 00:26:49.040
of his scalp to claim triumph. 
	
In the summer of 1880, relations between the Sioux&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:26:49.040 --> 00:26:55.040
and the Canadian authorities deteriorated further,&nbsp;
as the frequent cross-border raids by the Lakotas&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:26:55.040 --> 00:27:01.520
and others continued. The Canadian provincial&nbsp;
authorities had entered into clandestine talks&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:27:01.520 --> 00:27:06.960
with the Americans with the goal of transferring&nbsp;
Sitting Bull and his followers back south of the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:27:06.960 --> 00:27:14.240
border. By early 1881, while he and a group of&nbsp;
hardcore loyalists sat hungrily at their village&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:27:14.240 --> 00:27:19.680
on the frontier just near the Wood Mountain&nbsp;
police post, the majority of Sitting Bull’s&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:27:19.680 --> 00:27:26.160
people had already deserted him to go to live on&nbsp;
the Indian reservation at Fort Buford. Finally,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:27:26.160 --> 00:27:32.800
on the 12th of July 1881, Sitting Bull made&nbsp;
the agonising decision to forfeit his freedom&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:27:32.800 --> 00:27:38.240
after being convinced to do so by a trader by&nbsp;
the name of Jean Louis Legaré who had become&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:27:38.240 --> 00:27:44.800
friendly with the Lakota during their four-year&nbsp;
long Canadian exile. Eight days later, tired,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:27:44.800 --> 00:27:51.440
hungry and with rotting clothes, Sitting Bull and&nbsp;
nearly 200 followers entered the courtyard of Fort&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:27:51.440 --> 00:27:56.800
Buford. To mark the start of his imprisonment&nbsp;
the defeated rebel handed over his weapon to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:27:56.800 --> 00:28:03.520
the commanding officer and poignantly remarked: “I&nbsp;
wish it to be remembered that I was the last man&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:28:03.520 --> 00:28:11.840
of my tribe to surrender my rifle.”
Having handed himself in, Sitting Bull was&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:28:11.840 --> 00:28:16.240
promised by Major David Brotherton he would&nbsp;
spend the rest of his days at the Standing&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:28:16.240 --> 00:28:20.800
Rock Agency where most of his brethren&nbsp;
who had already surrendered were living,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:28:20.800 --> 00:28:25.200
but the higher-ups were soon starting&nbsp;
to reconsider. Worried that the defeated&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:28:25.200 --> 00:28:31.200
renegade could potentially become the centre of&nbsp;
renewed rebellion, he and his entourage were to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:28:31.200 --> 00:28:38.640
be delivered further up the Missouri River to Fort&nbsp;
Randall. Informed in September 1881 of the change,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:28:38.640 --> 00:28:45.600
Sitting Bull flew into a wild rage as his captors&nbsp;
had once again broken their promise. Nonetheless,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:28:45.600 --> 00:28:51.440
he and his group of followers were ushered on&nbsp;
board a steamer called the Sherman bound for Fort&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:28:51.440 --> 00:28:58.000
Randall where he spent twenty miserable months.&nbsp;
Seeing that the ageing commander posed little&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:28:58.000 --> 00:29:03.440
further threat to the government by then, several&nbsp;
army officers appealed to the Secretary of War,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:29:03.440 --> 00:29:10.480
Robert Lincoln, requesting to transfer them back&nbsp;
to Standing Rock. The petition worked and by the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:29:10.480 --> 00:29:17.520
end of April 1883 the captives were being boated&nbsp;
back down the Missouri River to Fort Yates and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:29:17.520 --> 00:29:24.720
the Standing Rock Agency. Disembarking&nbsp;
in May 1883 to Fort Yates, Sitting Bull,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:29:24.720 --> 00:29:31.120
who had begun to begrudgingly accept that the&nbsp;
old ways of his ancestors were dead, resolved&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:29:31.120 --> 00:29:37.680
to tow a middle line between preserving them and&nbsp;
adapting to the new life imposed on him. He would&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:29:37.680 --> 00:29:44.000
learn how to sow and harvest the land like his&nbsp;
jailers and he would even enrol his children in&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:29:44.000 --> 00:29:52.160
their education systems. At the same he would&nbsp;
still try to act as the Hunkpapa chief of old,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:29:52.160 --> 00:29:58.480
protecting his people from the injustices of the&nbsp;
new order. In this regard, his main nemesis over&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:29:58.480 --> 00:30:05.440
the coming years would be the overseer of Standing&nbsp;
Rock, James McLaughlin, a man who imposed order&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:30:05.440 --> 00:30:11.920
by keeping his subjects divided into squabbling&nbsp;
factions and by using the coercive power of ration&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:30:11.920 --> 00:30:18.960
control to achieve his aims. In an indication of&nbsp;
the dynamic that would define their relationship,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:30:18.960 --> 00:30:24.720
Sitting Bull told the agent upon his arrival&nbsp;
that, since he had no experience of doing it,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:30:24.720 --> 00:30:29.440
he would not be planting seeds this year&nbsp;
and that instead, he would look around&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:30:29.440 --> 00:30:36.320
to see how it was done before he tried it out.&nbsp;
MacLaughlin remarked that the chief was “pompous,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:30:36.320 --> 00:30:44.000
vain, and boastful” and a man “of very mediocre&nbsp;
ability, rather dull, and much the inferior of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:30:44.000 --> 00:30:52.560
his other lieutenants in intelligence.” 
In the spring of 1884 Sitting Bull settled&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:30:52.560 --> 00:30:58.240
on a spot south of the river on the reservation,&nbsp;
extremely close to where he had been born about 50&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:30:58.240 --> 00:31:04.800
years earlier. This would be his final residence.&nbsp;
Serious about adapting to the lifestyle of the new&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:31:04.800 --> 00:31:11.920
world, over the next couple of years his estate&nbsp;
swelled to 20 horses, 45 cattle, 80 chickens,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:31:11.920 --> 00:31:18.400
and fields full of oats, corn, and potatoes.&nbsp;
Sitting Bull had transformed easily enough from&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:31:18.400 --> 00:31:24.640
chief to farmer, while his adopted son, One Bull,&nbsp;
had even swapped the warrior lifestyle for a job&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:31:24.640 --> 00:31:30.960
in the local police force. In the meantime any&nbsp;
pretensions to power that Sitting Bull still had&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:31:30.960 --> 00:31:36.880
would be ended when, emphasising the importance&nbsp;
of his position as chief in front of a government&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:31:36.880 --> 00:31:43.120
committee called the Dawes Committee, one of&nbsp;
America’s most esteemed senators, John A. Logan,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:31:43.120 --> 00:31:48.560
gave him the most brutal dressing down of his&nbsp;
life when he replied: “You were not appointed by&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:31:48.560 --> 00:31:56.560
the Great Spirit. Appointments are not made that&nbsp;
way. You have no following, no power, no control,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:31:56.560 --> 00:32:03.280
and no right to any control. If it were not for&nbsp;
the government, you would be freezing and starving&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:32:03.280 --> 00:32:10.480
today in the mountains.” Yet, while his formal&nbsp;
rights of chief may have all but disintegrated,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:32:10.480 --> 00:32:16.640
Sitting Bull still had the power of his newfound&nbsp;
celebrity to fall back on. Despite the predicament&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:32:16.640 --> 00:32:22.400
he found himself in, the former vanquisher of&nbsp;
Custer became a subject of fascination for the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:32:22.400 --> 00:32:28.080
American public in his later years. He embarked&nbsp;
on a nationwide tour with the Buffalo Bill&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:32:28.080 --> 00:32:34.240
theatre troupe headed by the showman Bill Cody&nbsp;
during which, fully clad in traditional garb,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:32:34.240 --> 00:32:39.920
he smoked a pipe and cooked food while a presenter&nbsp;
delivered a lecture on the inner workings of the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:32:39.920 --> 00:32:45.200
Native Americans. He made a handsome amount&nbsp;
of money through his participations in one of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:32:45.200 --> 00:32:50.320
the world’s most famous entertainment troupes&nbsp;
in the late nineteenth century. Eventually,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:32:50.320 --> 00:32:55.120
though, Sitting Bull’s time in the limelight&nbsp;
was cut short and he was prevented from&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:32:55.120 --> 00:33:04.800
going out on tour again in 1886. 
After this, Sitting Bull led a quiet and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:33:04.800 --> 00:33:11.600
peaceful life at his homestead by the Grand River,&nbsp;
even sending a tomahawk to former Secretary of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:33:11.600 --> 00:33:18.800
the Interior Samuel J. Kirkwood as a gesture of&nbsp;
appreciation. That was until the passing of the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:33:18.800 --> 00:33:26.560
General Allotment Act of 1887 compelled him to&nbsp;
intervene on behalf of the Lakota once again. A&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:33:26.560 --> 00:33:33.280
law so horrific it even led to the reconciliation&nbsp;
of the Hunkpapa with their old rivals, the Crows,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:33:33.280 --> 00:33:40.640
the idea was that every native family was to&nbsp;
receive 160 acres or less of land in return&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:33:40.640 --> 00:33:48.080
for US citizenship. Once every indigenous family&nbsp;
had received their small parcel, any land that was&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:33:48.080 --> 00:33:55.280
left was to be bought at a cut-price 50 cents an&nbsp;
acre to encourage non Native American settlement.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:33:55.280 --> 00:34:00.720
The once boundless Sioux lands, reduced to the&nbsp;
size of a reservation, were to be subdivided&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:34:00.720 --> 00:34:06.960
into even smaller reservations. Although the&nbsp;
scheme was rejected by most of the Sioux,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:34:06.960 --> 00:34:12.160
through intimidation and manipulation the&nbsp;
government coerced three-quarters of the natives&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:34:12.160 --> 00:34:18.880
to accept the agreement by 1890. Thus, with the&nbsp;
last vestiges of their old life crumbling around&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:34:18.880 --> 00:34:26.400
them Native Americans turned to spirituality as&nbsp;
a bastion of opposition. Beginning in March 1890,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:34:26.400 --> 00:34:33.120
an indigenous holy man and self-proclaimed&nbsp;
messiah named Wovoka, claimed that by performing&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:34:33.120 --> 00:34:39.680
something called a Ghost Dance his people could be&nbsp;
transported to a perfect, pre-contact paradise of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:34:39.680 --> 00:34:45.200
peace, buffalo and untouched wilderness that&nbsp;
represented the world before the destructive&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:34:45.200 --> 00:34:52.640
arrival of the Europeans. Inspired by this, in&nbsp;
mid-1890, after a particularly poor harvest,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:34:52.640 --> 00:34:58.720
the Ghost Dance movement erupted across the&nbsp;
Standing Rock Reservation. Most worryingly for&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:34:58.720 --> 00:35:04.000
the men overseeing the land allotment, prophets&nbsp;
of the movement claimed that the Ghost Dance would&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:35:04.000 --> 00:35:10.080
even protect its acolytes from bullets. Sitting&nbsp;
Bull did not do anything to halt its spread,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:35:10.080 --> 00:35:16.400
as his people abandoned their cabins, set up&nbsp;
their tipis and engaged in the Ghost Dance.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:35:16.400 --> 00:35:21.280
He perhaps viewed this as an opportunity&nbsp;
to revitalise the power and influence that&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:35:21.280 --> 00:35:27.200
had been stripped away from him years earlier,&nbsp;
but equally, from McLaughlin’s viewpoint, this&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:35:27.200 --> 00:35:33.600
was a chance to finally take Sitting Bull down by&nbsp;
painting him as the high priest of a destabilising&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:35:33.600 --> 00:35:41.440
religious movement that was threatening the&nbsp;
newfound peace of the Great Plains.  
&nbsp;

00:35:41.440 --> 00:35:47.360
As the ghost dances assumed a more violent&nbsp;
undertone, President Benjamin Harrison ordered&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:35:47.360 --> 00:35:53.280
his Secretary of War to contain it if it got out&nbsp;
of hand, resulting in the arrival of troops to the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:35:53.280 --> 00:36:00.240
region on the 20th of November 1890 at the Pine&nbsp;
Ridge and Rosebud agencies near the Standing Rock&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:36:00.240 --> 00:36:06.480
reservation. As ominous clouds gathered, Sitting&nbsp;
Bull was reminded of a vision that had come to him&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:36:06.480 --> 00:36:12.080
soon after his transfer to Standing Rock from&nbsp;
Fort Randall in which a meadowlark had warned&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:36:12.080 --> 00:36:18.720
him that his own people, the Lakotas, would be the&nbsp;
ones that killed him. On the 27th of November, as&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:36:18.720 --> 00:36:25.520
people wildly flailed their bodies to and fro in&nbsp;
a dance circle on the Hunkpapa chief’s property,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:36:25.520 --> 00:36:32.240
Sitting Bull was visited by a Hunkpapa policeman&nbsp;
and one of McLaughlin’s favourites, Lone Man,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:36:32.240 --> 00:36:39.200
who on behalf of the reservation boss urged him to&nbsp;
tell his followers to stop with the ghost dances.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:36:39.200 --> 00:36:46.320
Sitting Bull replied that their ceremony harmed no&nbsp;
one, and that if the settlers had their religion&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:36:46.320 --> 00:36:52.560
his followers should be able to practice theirs&nbsp;
too in peace. By this time McLaughlin had already&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:36:52.560 --> 00:36:58.320
decided that, when the weather turned colder, he&nbsp;
was going to get his Lakota police to arrest their&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:36:58.320 --> 00:37:05.680
former leader, however the US military soon took&nbsp;
over the case. A Lakota policeman by the name of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:37:05.680 --> 00:37:11.760
Bull Head who had fought at Little Bighorn, was&nbsp;
tasked with spying on Sitting Bull after rumours&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:37:11.760 --> 00:37:16.800
emerged that he wanted to leave the Standing&nbsp;
Rock Agency to meet up with other ghost dancers&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:37:16.800 --> 00:37:23.680
at nearby Pine Ridge. Planting two spies into the&nbsp;
tribal council, they reported that Sitting Bull&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:37:23.680 --> 00:37:29.520
did in fact want to do this and that his followers&nbsp;
would shoot any policeman who tried to obstruct&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:37:29.520 --> 00:37:37.120
their passage. Thus, on the 15th of December&nbsp;
1890, at 6am, a squadron of Lakota policemen&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:37:37.120 --> 00:37:44.080
led by Bull Head burst into his cabin, wrestled&nbsp;
him to the ground, and walked him outside, but the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:37:44.080 --> 00:37:50.240
noise they had created had alerted Sitting Bull’s&nbsp;
entire settlement. Furious crowds implored them to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:37:50.240 --> 00:37:56.240
release him and surrounded the policemen as Bull&nbsp;
Head and another led their culprit to a police&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:37:56.240 --> 00:38:02.880
wagon, and then suddenly the sound of a Winchester&nbsp;
rifle rang through the air. Bull Head’s long-term&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:38:02.880 --> 00:38:10.000
rival, Catch-the-Bear, had shot him. As the Lakota&nbsp;
policemen fell to the ground he aimed his revolver&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:38:10.000 --> 00:38:16.800
upwards and fired off his own shot at Sitting&nbsp;
Bull. The chief caught the bullet in his chest,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:38:16.800 --> 00:38:22.320
and then an instant later he was killed by&nbsp;
a shot to the head by Bull Head’s underling,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:38:22.320 --> 00:38:28.880
Red Tomahawk. A point-blank firefight broke&nbsp;
out claiming the lives of Sitting Bull’s sons,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:38:28.880 --> 00:38:35.360
Jumping Bull and 17 year-old Crow Foot, and&nbsp;
grandson Chase-Them-Wounded, as well as five&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:38:35.360 --> 00:38:42.960
policemen. Sitting Bull was probably nearing&nbsp;
sixty years of age at the time of his death. 
&nbsp;

00:38:44.560 --> 00:38:51.120
Born into indigenous royalty, Sitting Bull was&nbsp;
one of the last generations to enjoy a typical&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:38:51.120 --> 00:38:57.520
native American childhood out on the Great Plains&nbsp;
amongst the buffalo herds. From an early age,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:38:57.520 --> 00:39:02.640
he displayed the qualities of a great leader,&nbsp;
earning a reputation not only for courage and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:39:02.640 --> 00:39:10.320
martial prowess, but also for generosity, humility&nbsp;
and spirituality. Proving his worth as a hardened&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:39:10.320 --> 00:39:16.880
warrior and skilled tactician in multiple battles,&nbsp;
he was appointed chief of the Hunkpapa tribe in&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:39:16.880 --> 00:39:24.000
the 1850s as the threat of settler expansion&nbsp;
picked up speed. Over the next decade or so,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:39:24.000 --> 00:39:29.280
he dedicated himself to preserving the&nbsp;
independence of his people and fought valiantly&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:39:29.280 --> 00:39:36.480
to defend the lands of the Hunkpapa from westward&nbsp;
US expansion. In the late 1860s this indomitable&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:39:36.480 --> 00:39:42.960
spirit of resistance, unmatched among his peers,&nbsp;
was recognised when he was made head chief of the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:39:42.960 --> 00:39:48.800
Sioux Confederation. By this point having seen&nbsp;
enough action to know that his enemy’s weapons&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:39:48.800 --> 00:39:55.680
and tactics were superior to his own, he decided&nbsp;
to switch to a more defensive posture. After&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:39:55.680 --> 00:40:01.120
repelling an incursion by railway companies&nbsp;
looking to lay track down in his territory,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:40:01.120 --> 00:40:06.160
Sitting Bull illustrated to the American&nbsp;
government that he would never surrender.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:40:06.160 --> 00:40:13.040
During the Great Sioux War of 1876 his astonishing&nbsp;
victory over General George Custer at the Battle&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:40:13.040 --> 00:40:19.600
of Little Bighorn made him the most prominent&nbsp;
and feared rebel in the United States. But it&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:40:19.600 --> 00:40:26.160
was a short-lived victory. It ultimately led to an&nbsp;
American offensive so devastating that the Sioux&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:40:26.160 --> 00:40:32.080
Confederacy was broken up and Sitting Bull and his&nbsp;
hungry band had no choice but to flee to Canada&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:40:32.080 --> 00:40:38.240
for four years. Eventually the disappearance&nbsp;
of buffalo there and rising diplomatic tensions&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:40:38.240 --> 00:40:43.680
between Canada and the United States, compelled&nbsp;
Sitting Bull and what remained of his followers&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:40:43.680 --> 00:40:50.160
to join their brothers and sisters at Standing&nbsp;
Rock Agency. There he tried to act as a shield&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:40:50.160 --> 00:40:55.920
of his people while also adapting to modern&nbsp;
American life. While flourishing as a farmer&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:40:55.920 --> 00:41:02.080
and taking advantage of his famous name to make&nbsp;
money on various nationwide tours, deep down&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:41:02.080 --> 00:41:09.040
Sitting Bull was a lost soul yearning for the days&nbsp;
of old. The arrival of the Ghost Dance movement,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:41:09.040 --> 00:41:16.000
with its emphasis on a pre-contact utopia,&nbsp;
filled him with purpose once more but it also&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:41:16.000 --> 00:41:22.080
spelled his downfall. Viewing it as a threat&nbsp;
to the established order, the military tasked&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:41:22.080 --> 00:41:27.440
Lakota policemen with apprehending him. The&nbsp;
tragedy was that the greatest of the Sioux&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:41:27.440 --> 00:41:37.040
chiefs died fighting his own people. 
What do you think of Sitting Bull? Was he the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:41:37.040 --> 00:41:42.480
last great native American chief who bravely&nbsp;
resisted US government encroachments on the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:41:42.480 --> 00:41:49.120
lands of tribes like the Hunkpapa, or was&nbsp;
his cause doomed from the beginning? Please&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:41:49.120 --> 00:42:09.520
let us know in the comment section and in the&nbsp;
meantime thank you very much for watching.

